1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,880
 In the previous episode, we explored what makes AI-native systems

2
00:00:04,880 --> 00:00:10,000
 different. We looked at agents, reasoning, conversations and workflows,

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:13,850
 and began to see how intelligence is becoming another architecture

4
00:00:13,850 --> 00:00:15,000
 building block.

5
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:18,910
 But before we continue that journey, I want to pause for a moment

6
00:00:18,910 --> 00:00:22,000
 because AI-native systems are still systems.

7
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:26,600
 It's easy to become fascinated by large language models, prompts,

8
00:00:26,600 --> 00:00:30,760
 agents, but none of them replace the fundamentals of software

9
00:00:30,760 --> 00:00:32,000
 engineering.

10
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:35,650
 Whatever system application or AI assistant you're building, every

11
00:00:35,650 --> 00:00:39,940
 production system is ultimately judged by the same question. Can people

12
00:00:39,940 --> 00:00:41,000
 trust it?

13
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:45,860
 And that's what this episode is about, the characteristics that make

14
00:00:45,860 --> 00:00:50,630
 systems successful. Many of these qualities have been part of good

15
00:00:50,630 --> 00:00:52,000
 architecture.

16
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:56,000
 AI doesn't replace them, it amplifies them.

17
00:00:56,000 --> 00:00:59,950
 When architects think about software, they tend to think in two

18
00:00:59,950 --> 00:01:04,000
 dimensions. The first is functionality, what does the system do?

19
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:09,310
 And the second, most importantly, is characteristics. How well does it

20
00:01:09,310 --> 00:01:14,000
 do it? Imagine asking an AI assistant to design a solution architecture.

21
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:19,340
 Producing that architecture is functionality. Whether the response is

22
00:01:19,340 --> 00:01:23,890
 secure, reliable, observable, performant and trustworthy describes the

23
00:01:23,890 --> 00:01:27,000
 characteristics of the system delivering it.

24
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:31,320
 These qualities determine whether a system succeeds in production far

25
00:01:31,320 --> 00:01:37,300
 more than its features. Features attract users, characteristics keep

26
00:01:37,300 --> 00:01:38,000
 them.

27
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:43,100
 Throughout my career, one lesson has remained remarkably consistent.

28
00:01:43,100 --> 00:01:46,000
 Architectural patterns don't exist for their own sake.

29
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:54,190
 We don't choose CQRS because it's fashionable, we don't adopt event

30
00:01:54,190 --> 00:01:59,310
 sourcing because it's interesting. We choose these patterns because

31
00:01:59,310 --> 00:02:00,000
 they improve one or more qualities of the system we're building.

32
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:04,250
 Every architecture decision is really a trade-off. Improving one

33
00:02:04,250 --> 00:02:08,000
 characteristic often makes another one more difficult.

34
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:12,470
 Architecture is the art of deciding which qualities matter most for the

35
00:02:12,470 --> 00:02:15,000
 problem that you're trying to solve.

36
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:19,430
 That hasn't changed because AI has arrived, if anything, it makes it

37
00:02:19,430 --> 00:02:21,000
 even more important.

38
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:26,750
 Trust is the foundation of every production system. Traditionally, that

39
00:02:26,750 --> 00:02:33,000
 meant authentication, authorization, security, privacy and compliance.

40
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:37,930
 AI native systems introduce another layer of questions. Which models

41
00:02:37,930 --> 00:02:42,360
 should the user be allowed to access? What information is safe to

42
00:02:42,360 --> 00:02:44,000
 include in prompts?

43
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:48,670
 Can sensitive information leak into a model? Should an agent be allowed

44
00:02:48,670 --> 00:02:52,000
 to call this tool? Can every decision be audited?

45
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:57,100
 Security is no longer about protecting APIs. It's about protecting the

46
00:02:57,100 --> 00:03:02,110
 intelligence. An agent might be technically capable of performing an

47
00:03:02,110 --> 00:03:03,000
 action. It doesn't mean it's authorized to perform it.

48
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:09,620
 The architecture might make that distinction. Trust has to exist across

49
00:03:09,620 --> 00:03:14,300
 the entire journey. From user intent, through to reasoning, to tool

50
00:03:14,300 --> 00:03:17,000
 execution, to the final result.

51
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:23,000
 Reliability answers a simple question. Can users depend on a system?

52
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:27,680
 Traditionally, that means availability, resilience, fault tolerance and

53
00:03:27,680 --> 00:03:28,000
 recovery.

54
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:33,500
 AI native systems introduce new failure modes. Models may become

55
00:03:33,500 --> 00:03:39,890
 unavailable. Providers may rate limit request. Retrieval services may

56
00:03:39,890 --> 00:03:44,000
 fail. External tools might stop responding.

57
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:48,440
 Sometimes every technical component succeeds, yet the answer is still

58
00:03:48,440 --> 00:03:53,460
 poor. A reliable AI native system should continue operating even when

59
00:03:53,460 --> 00:03:57,000
 parts of its intelligence become unavailable.

60
00:03:57,000 --> 00:04:01,930
 That may mean selecting another model, retrying an operation, pausing a

61
00:04:01,930 --> 00:04:07,030
 workflow, requesting human intervention or continuing with deterministic

62
00:04:07,030 --> 00:04:09,000
 functionality instead.

63
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:13,710
 Reliability isn't about preventing failure. It's about deciding what

64
00:04:13,710 --> 00:04:17,000
 should happen when failure inevitably occurs.

65
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:23,000
 Performance is much more about response time. It's about latency,

66
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:27,680
 throughput, resource utilization and cost. One interesting

67
00:04:27,680 --> 00:04:31,490
 characteristic of AI native systems is that they often promise

68
00:04:31,490 --> 00:04:35,000
 perceived performance rather than absolute performance.

69
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:43,170
 A streamed response that starts immediately usually fails faster than

70
00:04:43,170 --> 00:04:49,000
 waiting several seconds for a complete answer. But streaming doesn't

71
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:49,000
 remove latency. It simply changes how users experience it.

72
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:54,100
 Performance also has to consider the entire reasoning pipeline. From

73
00:04:54,100 --> 00:04:59,680
 building context, receiving knowledge, selecting tools, calling those

74
00:04:59,680 --> 00:05:04,950
 tools and coordinating agents, then running the evaluations. Each stage

75
00:05:04,950 --> 00:05:10,000
 contributes to the overall experience, and each stage has a cost.

76
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:15,150
 Architecture determines whether expensive reasoning is worthwhile and

77
00:05:15,150 --> 00:05:20,000
 whether deterministic software can do the job more efficiently.

78
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:24,300
 When architects hear the word scalability, we usually think about

79
00:05:24,300 --> 00:05:29,460
 infrastructure with horizontal scaling, queues, distributed processing,

80
00:05:29,460 --> 00:05:33,650
 background workers and petitioning. Those ideas remain just as

81
00:05:33,650 --> 00:05:36,000
 important in AI native systems.

82
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:42,640
 Inference is expensive. Retrieval consumes resources. Long running workflows

83
00:05:42,640 --> 00:05:47,680
 occupy capacity. Designing systems at scale economically is now just as

84
00:05:47,680 --> 00:05:52,000
 important as designing systems at scale technically.

85
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:56,230
 But AI introduces another dimension. We're no longer just scaling

86
00:05:56,230 --> 00:06:01,000
 infrastructure. We're scaling intelligence itself.

87
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:04,990
 One approach is to make an individual agent more capable, give it more

88
00:06:04,990 --> 00:06:09,150
 tools and memory and knowledge, more instructions and a larger context

89
00:06:09,150 --> 00:06:10,000
 window.

90
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:15,260
 Initially, this feels attractive. The architecture appears simple. One

91
00:06:15,260 --> 00:06:20,680
 agent understands the requests and makes decisions and completes the

92
00:06:20,680 --> 00:06:21,000
 work.

93
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:25,600
 Essentially, that simplicity starts working against us. The context

94
00:06:25,600 --> 00:06:30,590
 becomes enormous. The prompt grows. The tool catalog expands. The model

95
00:06:30,590 --> 00:06:35,000
 has to work harder simply to understand everything we've given it.

96
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:43,920
 Latency increases. Costs increase. Reasoning becomes less focused.

97
00:06:43,920 --> 00:06:52,050
 Important information becomes diluted amongst everything else. A larger

98
00:06:52,050 --> 00:06:52,590
 context window doesn't automatically produce better reasoning. An agent

99
00:06:52,590 --> 00:06:54,850
 can become more capable while becoming harder to understand, harder to

100
00:06:54,850 --> 00:06:58,000
 evaluate and harder to operate.

101
00:06:58,000 --> 00:07:03,490
 The alternative is to scale out. Instead of creating one increasingly

102
00:07:03,490 --> 00:07:09,040
 capable agent, we create several specialized agents. One retrieves

103
00:07:09,040 --> 00:07:12,800
 knowledge, another evaluates the evidence, another one checks

104
00:07:12,800 --> 00:07:14,000
 architectural principles,

105
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:20,660
 another one explores trade-offs, each agent has a narrower

106
00:07:20,660 --> 00:07:29,000
 responsibility, a smaller context window, and more focused purpose.

107
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:30,640
 This often improves clarity, isolation and evaluation. But scaling out

108
00:07:30,640 --> 00:07:31,000
 isn't free.

109
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:37,000
 The complexity doesn't disappear. It simply moves elsewhere. Now the

110
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:42,000
 architecture has to decide which agent should receive the request.

111
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:46,000
 Whether several agents are needed, how context should be transferred,

112
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:49,000
 how results should be combined,

113
00:07:49,000 --> 00:07:53,280
 where workflow state should live, how failures should be recovered, and

114
00:07:53,280 --> 00:07:57,360
 how the entire reasoning process should be observed. Does this sound

115
00:07:57,360 --> 00:08:01,740
 familiar? It should. It's exactly the trade-off we encountered when

116
00:08:01,740 --> 00:08:05,000
 software evolved from monoliths to distributed systems.

117
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:13,170
 We exchanged complexity inside one component for complexity across many

118
00:08:13,170 --> 00:08:20,140
 components, and the same thing's happening with agents. So, the

119
00:08:20,140 --> 00:08:21,400
 architectural question isn't whether agents should be large or small.

120
00:08:21,400 --> 00:08:22,000
 It's where we want the complexity to live.

121
00:08:22,000 --> 00:08:27,230
 Scaling up concentrates complexity inside the reasoning of one agent.

122
00:08:27,230 --> 00:08:32,680
 Scaling out distributes complexity across the architecture. Neither

123
00:08:32,680 --> 00:08:36,300
 approach is universally correct. Some problems benefit from richer

124
00:08:36,300 --> 00:08:39,000
 reasoning inside a single coherent agent.

125
00:08:39,000 --> 00:08:43,500
 Others benefit from collaboration between several specialized agents.

126
00:08:43,500 --> 00:08:47,770
 Many systems will combine both. But the goal isn't to build the biggest

127
00:08:47,770 --> 00:08:52,230
 possible agent, nor is it to build the greatest number of agents. The

128
00:08:52,230 --> 00:08:57,210
 goal is to place complexity where it is understood, controlled and

129
00:08:57,210 --> 00:08:58,000
 operated.

130
00:08:58,000 --> 00:09:03,150
 This leads to an important architectural principle. An agent shouldn't

131
00:09:03,150 --> 00:09:07,570
 be small for the sake of being small. It should be coherent. Its

132
00:09:07,570 --> 00:09:13,000
 responsibilities, context and tools should naturally belong together.

133
00:09:13,000 --> 00:09:18,300
 Splitting a coherent responsibility across many tiny agents can create

134
00:09:18,300 --> 00:09:21,000
 more complexity than it removes.

135
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:26,610
 It's a very similar to prematurely decomposing the system into microservices.

136
00:09:26,610 --> 00:09:31,680
 Each service becomes smaller. But the overall system becomes harder to

137
00:09:31,680 --> 00:09:36,000
 understand because communication and coordination dominate the design.

138
00:09:36,000 --> 00:09:43,860
 Every agent boundary also becomes a routing boundary, a context

139
00:09:43,860 --> 00:09:52,340
 boundary, a trust boundary, an evaluation boundary and also a failure

140
00:09:52,340 --> 00:09:54,860
 boundary. Agents should be split when the additional responsibility

141
00:09:54,860 --> 00:09:56,000
 makes them harder to understand, secure, evaluate, observe and evolve.

142
00:09:56,000 --> 00:10:03,860
 The important characteristic isn't size, it's clarity. Observability

143
00:10:03,860 --> 00:10:08,030
 has become one of the defining characteristics of modern software. Traditionally

144
00:10:08,030 --> 00:10:11,000
 we think about logs, metrics and traces.

145
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:16,920
 AI native systems require much higher visibility. We need to understand

146
00:10:16,920 --> 00:10:22,920
 prompt versions, model selections and token usage, retrieval quality,

147
00:10:22,920 --> 00:10:28,250
 tool calls and workflow execution, confidence scores and evaluation

148
00:10:28,250 --> 00:10:30,000
 results and user feedback.

149
00:10:30,000 --> 00:10:33,990
 In a multi-agent system we also need to understand why a particular

150
00:10:33,990 --> 00:10:38,110
 agent was selected, what context it received, what information it

151
00:10:38,110 --> 00:10:42,100
 ignored and how it reached its conclusion. Observability is no longer

152
00:10:42,100 --> 00:10:46,000
 just about understanding software execution. It's about understanding

153
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:49,000
 the intelligence behavior.

154
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:54,380
 Technology changes, AI changes even faster. Good architecture should

155
00:10:54,380 --> 00:10:59,740
 make change easier rather than harder. Models and prompting techniques

156
00:10:59,740 --> 00:11:01,000
 will evolve.

157
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:07,950
 Agent frameworks and retrieval techniques will evolve. Architectural

158
00:11:07,950 --> 00:11:15,550
 intent should survive all of those implementation changes. Responsibilities

159
00:11:15,550 --> 00:11:19,660
 shouldn't exist inside prompts. They should exist independently of the

160
00:11:19,660 --> 00:11:21,000
 models that implement them. Architecture should make placement possible

161
00:11:21,000 --> 00:11:24,000
 rather than expensive.

162
00:11:24,000 --> 00:11:29,100
 Building software is only part of the challenge. Someone has to operate

163
00:11:29,100 --> 00:11:34,680
 it, whether it's configuration, monitoring, diagnostics, deployments,

164
00:11:34,680 --> 00:11:37,000
 rollback and incident response.

165
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:42,150
 These all remain essential. AI native systems introduce new operational

166
00:11:42,150 --> 00:11:47,680
 concerns. Changing routing policies, disabling models, inspecting

167
00:11:47,680 --> 00:11:52,300
 reasoning paths, replaying workflows, replacing prompts and comparing

168
00:11:52,300 --> 00:11:54,000
 evaluation results.

169
00:11:54,000 --> 00:12:00,940
 As systems scale out, operational complexity increases. Architecture

170
00:12:00,940 --> 00:12:07,000
 has to balance logical separation with practical operability.

171
00:12:07,000 --> 00:12:10,940
 Traditional software engineering gives us testing, unit tests,

172
00:12:10,940 --> 00:12:14,000
 integration, contract and performance tests.

173
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:18,510
 These tell us whether the software behaves correctly. Large language

174
00:12:18,510 --> 00:12:23,490
 models introduce something different. They are probabilistic. The same

175
00:12:23,490 --> 00:12:28,150
 prompt may produce different but equally valid answers. A workflow may

176
00:12:28,150 --> 00:12:31,000
 execute perfectly while producing a poor response.

177
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:36,960
 Testing is essential but it's no longer sufficient. Machine learning

178
00:12:36,960 --> 00:12:42,630
 has always relied on evaluation. Rather than asking whether software

179
00:12:42,630 --> 00:12:46,000
 works, it asks how well the model performs.

180
00:12:46,000 --> 00:12:51,610
 AI native systems inherit that mindset. We need to ask questions like,

181
00:12:51,610 --> 00:12:57,420
 was the answer correct? Was it grounded in trusted information? Did the

182
00:12:57,420 --> 00:13:02,000
 agent choose the right tools? Did it satisfy the user's intent?

183
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:07,960
 Was the behavior safe? Was the outcome worth the cost? Testing verifies

184
00:13:07,960 --> 00:13:13,760
 the implementation, evaluation measures behavior. Both are equally

185
00:13:13,760 --> 00:13:15,000
 important.

186
00:13:15,000 --> 00:13:19,310
 Multi-agent systems introduce another level of evaluation. We're no

187
00:13:19,310 --> 00:13:23,130
 longer evaluating just the final answer. We're evaluating the

188
00:13:23,130 --> 00:13:25,000
 architecture that produced it.

189
00:13:25,000 --> 00:13:29,660
 Did the router select the correct agent? Did each agent receive the

190
00:13:29,660 --> 00:13:34,250
 right context? Did agents duplicate work? Did one introduce incorrect

191
00:13:34,250 --> 00:13:39,000
 information? Was collaboration actually beneficial?

192
00:13:39,000 --> 00:13:47,700
 More agents don't automatically produce a better system. They

193
00:13:47,700 --> 00:13:57,000
 additionally, their additional complexity has to justify as well. The

194
00:13:57,000 --> 00:13:57,000
 quality of collaboration matters far more than the number of agents

195
00:13:57,000 --> 00:13:57,000
 involved.

196
00:13:57,000 --> 00:14:06,020
 One question sits underneath every evaluation framework. What does

197
00:14:06,020 --> 00:14:10,000
 success actually look like? Traditional software answers that with

198
00:14:10,000 --> 00:14:10,000
 assertions. Machine learning uses metrics.

199
00:14:10,000 --> 00:14:17,500
 I believe AI native systems need another layer. Capabilities. A

200
00:14:17,500 --> 00:14:25,950
 capability describes intent, expected outcomes, policies, constraints

201
00:14:25,950 --> 00:14:29,000
 and measures of success. Instead of evaluating whether an agent

202
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:29,000
 produced a particular sentence, we evaluate whether the capability was

203
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:29,000
 successfully delivered.

204
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:33,010
 This is one of the motivations behind the declarative capability

205
00:14:33,010 --> 00:14:37,700
 language, or DCL. By explicitly describing success, we can evaluate

206
00:14:37,700 --> 00:14:41,000
 outcomes rather than the implementation details.

207
00:14:41,000 --> 00:14:46,440
 One thing that has become increasingly clear to me while researching AI

208
00:14:46,440 --> 00:14:52,510
 native systems, the fundamentals haven't changed. Architecture is still

209
00:14:52,510 --> 00:14:57,900
 about balancing competing system qualities. Security becomes more

210
00:14:57,900 --> 00:15:00,740
 complex. Observability becomes richer. Reliability becomes more

211
00:15:00,740 --> 00:15:01,000
 challenging.

212
00:15:01,000 --> 00:15:06,040
 Scalability extends beyond infrastructure into the architecture of

213
00:15:06,040 --> 00:15:11,680
 intelligence itself. Testing is complemented by evaluation. We're still

214
00:15:11,680 --> 00:15:15,860
 solving the same architectural problems. We're simply solving them in

215
00:15:15,860 --> 00:15:20,000
 systems that can now reason, collaborate and make decisions.

216
00:15:20,000 --> 00:15:25,100
 Before designing any architecture is worth asking two simple questions.

217
00:15:25,100 --> 00:15:29,990
 What must the system do? And what properties must the system possess?

218
00:15:29,990 --> 00:15:34,000
 The first defines functionality, the second shapes the architecture.

219
00:15:34,000 --> 00:15:39,100
 AI native systems introduce remarkably new capabilities, but they

220
00:15:39,100 --> 00:15:44,680
 simply don't remove the need for careful architectural thinking. If

221
00:15:44,680 --> 00:15:49,460
 anything, they make it even more important. In the next episode, I'll

222
00:15:49,460 --> 00:15:53,090
 step away from discussing system characteristics and explain how I

223
00:15:53,090 --> 00:15:56,880
 personally approach solution design and how I evolve architecture over

224
00:15:56,880 --> 00:15:57,000
 time.

225
00:15:57,000 --> 00:16:01,620
 Rather than presenting a fixed methodology, I'll share the process I

226
00:16:01,620 --> 00:16:06,570
 follow from understanding the problem through architectural thinking to

227
00:16:06,570 --> 00:16:11,000
 evolving solution that can adapt as our understanding grows.

